questions.explanation:
### Approach: Doubly Linked List + Hash Map

We need O(1) `get` and `set`. A hash map gives O(1) lookups, but we also need to track usage order. A **doubly linked list** lets us move nodes to the front (mark as recently used) and remove from the tail (evict LRU) in O(1).

### Solution

```javascript
class LinkedListNode {
  constructor(key, val, prev = null, next = null) {
    this.key = key;
    this.val = val;
    this.prev = prev;
    this.next = next;
  }
}

class LRUCache {
  constructor(capacity) {
    this.capacity = capacity;
    this.nodesMap = {};
    this.size = 0;
    this.head = new LinkedListNode();
    this.tail = new LinkedListNode();
  }

  get(key) {
    if (!(key in this.nodesMap)) return undefined;
    const node = this.nodesMap[key];
    this._promote(node);
    return node.val;
  }

  set(key, val) {
    if (key in this.nodesMap) {
      const node = this.nodesMap[key];
      node.val = val;
      this._promote(node);
    } else {
      const node = new LinkedListNode(key, val);
      this._append(node);
    }
  }

  _promote(node) {
    this._evict(node);
    this._append(node);
  }

  _append(node) {
    this.nodesMap[node.key] = node;

    if (!this.head.next) {
      this.head.next = node;
      this.tail.prev = node;
      node.prev = this.head;
      node.next = this.tail;
    } else {
      const oldTail = this.tail.prev;
      oldTail.next = node;
      node.prev = oldTail;
      node.next = this.tail;
      this.tail.prev = node;
    }

    this.size += 1;

    if (this.size > this.capacity) {
      this._evict(this.head.next);
    }
  }

  _evict(node) {
    delete this.nodesMap[node.key];
    this.size -= 1;

    const prevNode = node.prev;
    const nextNode = node.next;

    if (prevNode === this.head && nextNode === this.tail) {
      this.head.next = null;
      this.tail.prev = null;
      this.size = 0;
      return;
    }

    if (prevNode === this.head) {
      nextNode.prev = this.head;
      this.head.next = nextNode;
      return;
    }

    if (nextNode === this.tail) {
      prevNode.next = this.tail;
      this.tail.prev = prevNode;
      return;
    }

    prevNode.next = nextNode;
    nextNode.prev = prevNode;
  }
}
```

### Alternative: JavaScript Map (Simpler)

Since `Map` preserves insertion order, we can use it directly:

```javascript
class LRUCache {
  constructor(capacity) {
    this.capacity = capacity;
    this.items = new Map();
  }

  get(key) {
    if (!this.items.has(key)) return undefined;
    const val = this.items.get(key);
    this.items.delete(key);
    this.items.set(key, val);
    return val;
  }

  set(key, val) {
    this.items.delete(key);
    this.items.set(key, val);
    if (this.items.size > this.capacity) {
      const lruKey = this.items.keys().next().value;
      this.items.delete(lruKey);
    }
  }
}
```

### Key Points

- The doubly-linked-list approach combines a **hash map** for O(1) lookup with a **linked list** for O(1) reordering/eviction
- Use **dummy head and tail nodes** to simplify edge cases (first/last node operations)
- On `get`: promote the node to the tail (most recently used)
- On `set` for existing key: update value + promote
- On `set` for new key: append to tail, then evict head.next if over capacity
- The Map-based solution is simpler but both are valid; interviewers often expect the linked-list implementation

questions.description:
Design and implement an `AsyncTaskQueue` class that manages execution of asynchronous tasks with a specified maximum concurrency limit.

The queue should:
- Execute tasks in the order they are added (FIFO)
- Ensure no more than the specified number of tasks run concurrently
- Silently ignore task rejections — the queue must continue processing

### API

```javascript
class AsyncTaskQueue {
  constructor(concurrency) {}
  // Add an async task to the queue.
  // Returns a Promise that resolves when the task completes
  // (resolves even if the task rejects — rejections are silently ignored).
  queue(task) {}
}
```

### Example

```javascript
const queue = new AsyncTaskQueue(2);

const task1 = () =>
  new Promise((resolve) => setTimeout(() => resolve("Task 1 done"), 1000));
const task2 = () =>
  new Promise((_, reject) => setTimeout(() => reject("Task 2 failed"), 500));
const task3 = () =>
  new Promise((resolve) => setTimeout(() => resolve("Task 3 done"), 200));

queue.queue(task1); // Starts immediately
queue.queue(task2); // Starts immediately (concurrency = 2)
queue.queue(task3); // Waits until one of the first two completes
// Task 2's failure is silently ignored; Task 3 still runs
```

Complete the implementation in the editor.

questions.template:
class AsyncTaskQueue {
  constructor(concurrency) {
    // Write your code here
  }

  queue(task) {
    // Write your code here
  }
}

questions.explanation:
### Approach: Queue with Active Counter

Maintain a **task queue** and a **pending count**. When `queue()` is called, push the task, then `flush()` starts work if capacity allows. Each task runs; when it settles (success or failure), decrement pending and call `flush()` again to drain the queue.

### Solution

```javascript
class AsyncTaskQueue {
  constructor(concurrency) {
    this.limit = concurrency;
    this.pending = 0;
    this.taskQueue = [];
  }

  queue(task) {
    return new Promise((resolve) => {
      this.taskQueue.push(() => {
        this.pending++;
        task()
          .then(resolve)
          .catch(() => resolve())
          .finally(() => {
            this.pending--;
            this.flush();
          });
      });
      this.flush();
    });
  }

  flush() {
    while (this.pending < this.limit && this.taskQueue.length) {
      const cb = this.taskQueue.shift();
      cb();
    }
  }
}
```

### Key Points
- `queue()` returns a Promise that resolves when the task completes — **always resolves**, even if the task rejects (rejections are caught and swallowed via `.catch(() => resolve())`)
- `flush()` is the gatekeeper — it starts new tasks as long as `pending < limit` and the queue has work
- The `.finally()` block ensures `pending` decrements and `flush()` is called regardless of success/failure
- Tasks run FIFO: `this.taskQueue.shift()` removes from the front
- A `while` loop in `flush()` starts as many tasks as capacity allows (useful if multiple tasks complete simultaneously)
- The `pending` counter tracks in-flight tasks — simple and avoids time-based polling
- Alternative worker-pool pattern (create K workers that each pull from a shared index) also works well

questions.template:
function throttlePromises(funcs, limit) {
  // Write your code here
}

questions.explanation:
### Approach: explicit scheduler

Track three counters — `nextIndex` (next task to start), `running` (in-flight promises), and `completed` (settled tasks). The `run()` function fills available slots up to `limit`, and each task's `finally` block decrements `running`, increments `completed`, and calls `run()` again to start more work.

### Solution

```javascript
function throttlePromises(funcs, limit) {
  return new Promise((resolve, reject) => {
    const results = new Array(funcs.length);

    let nextIndex = 0;
    let completed = 0;
    let running = 0;

    function run() {
      // all tasks are finished
      if (completed === funcs.length) {
        resolve(results);
        return;
      }

      // fill available slots
      while (running < limit && nextIndex < funcs.length) {
        const current = nextIndex++;

        running++;

        Promise.resolve(funcs[current]())
          .then((value) => {
            results[current] = value;
          })
          .catch(reject)
          .finally(() => {
            running--;
            completed++;
            run();
          });
      }
    }

    run();
  });
}
```

### Example walkthrough

```javascript
const sleep = (time, value) =>
  new Promise((resolve) => setTimeout(() => resolve(value), time));

const tasks = [
  () => sleep(300, "A"),
  () => sleep(100, "B"),
  () => sleep(200, "C"),
  () => sleep(50, "D"),
];

throttlePromises(tasks, 2).then(console.log);
// start A, start B
// finish B → start C
// finish A → start D
// finish D, finish C
// ["A", "B", "C", "D"]
```

### Key points

- **`run()` is the driver** — called on start and after every settlement to fill empty concurrency slots.
- **Order vs execution** — tasks may finish out of order, but results are stored by index so the output matches `Promise.all`.
- **Reject fast** — `.catch(reject)` relays the first error to the outer promise.
- **Empty input** — `completed === funcs.length` is `0 === 0` on the first `run()` call, so it resolves `[]` immediately.
- **Time/space** — O(n) time and O(n) space.

### Alternative: worker pool

```javascript
function throttlePromises(funcs, limit) {
  const results = new Array(funcs.length);
  let next = 0;

  async function worker() {
    while (next < funcs.length) {
      const current = next++;
      results[current] = await funcs[current]();
    }
  }

  return Promise.all(
    Array(Math.min(limit, funcs.length)).fill(0).map(worker),
  ).then(() => results);
}
```

Shorter and easy to reason about in interviews — same O(n) behavior.

questions.description:
Implement `groupBy(items, keyFn)` that groups an array into an object keyed by the result of `keyFn`.

Each value is an array of the items that produced that key, in their original order. An empty input produces an empty object.

### Example

```javascript
const users = [
  { name: "Ada", role: "admin" },
  { name: "Bo", role: "member" },
  { name: "Cy", role: "admin" },
];

groupBy(users, (u) => u.role);
// { admin: [{name:"Ada",...}, {name:"Cy",...}], member: [{name:"Bo",...}] }
```

This backs any "group the results by category" requirement in a project question.

Complete the implementation in the editor.

questions.template:
function groupBy(items, keyFn) {
  // Write your code here
}

questions.explanation:
### Solution

```javascript
function groupBy(items, keyFn) {
  return items.reduce((acc, item) => {
    const key = keyFn(item);
    (acc[key] ??= []).push(item);
    return acc;
  }, {});
}
```

### How it works

One `reduce` pass over the array. For each item, compute its key, lazily create the bucket if it does not exist, and push. Because we push in iteration order, items keep their original relative order within each group.

`(acc[key] ??= []).push(item)` is the compact form of:

```javascript
if (!acc[key]) acc[key] = [];
acc[key].push(item);
```

### Key points

- `??=` assigns only when the left side is `null` or `undefined` — safer than `||=` if a falsy value could be a legitimate bucket.
- Keys are coerced to strings, so `keyFn` returning `1` and `"1"` collide.
- Modern runtimes ship `Object.groupBy(items, keyFn)` natively, but you should be able to write it.
- Returning `{}` for an empty array falls out of `reduce` naturally — no special case needed.

questions.template:
function sortBy(items, keys) {
  // Write your code here
}

questions.explanation:
### Solution

```javascript
function sortBy(items, keys) {
  return [...items].sort((a, b) => {
    for (const { key, dir } of keys) {
      const av = a[key];
      const bv = b[key];

      let cmp;
      if (typeof av === "number" && typeof bv === "number") {
        cmp = av - bv;
      } else {
        cmp = String(av).localeCompare(String(bv));
      }

      if (cmp !== 0) return dir === "desc" ? -cmp : cmp;
    }
    return 0;
  });
}
```

### How it works

The comparator walks the key list in priority order. The first key that produces a non-zero comparison decides the ordering; ties fall through to the next key. If every key ties, return `0` and the relative order is preserved (`Array.prototype.sort` is stable in modern engines).

Direction is applied by negating the comparison rather than duplicating the branch.

### Key points

- **`[...items]` first.** `.sort()` mutates in place. Sorting a prop array in React mutates the parent's data and, because the reference is unchanged, may not even trigger a re-render.
- **Branch on type.** Comparing numbers as strings gives `"10" < "9"` — silently wrong ordering.
- `localeCompare` handles accents and case more sensibly than `<`.
- A shorter two-key version reads nicely with `||`, since a zero comparison is falsy:

```javascript
arr.sort((a, b) => a.last.localeCompare(b.last) || a.age - b.age);
```

questions.description:
Implement `paginate(items, page, pageSize)` returning the slice of `items` for a **1-indexed** page, plus pagination metadata.

Return an object:

```javascript
{ items, page, totalPages, totalItems, hasPrev, hasNext }
```

Clamp `page` into the valid range rather than returning an empty slice for an out-of-range page. An empty input has `totalPages: 1`.

### Example

```javascript
paginate([1, 2, 3, 4, 5], 2, 2);
// { items: [3, 4], page: 2, totalPages: 3, totalItems: 5, hasPrev: true, hasNext: true }
```

Complete the implementation in the editor.

questions.template:
function paginate(items, page, pageSize) {
  // Write your code here
}

questions.explanation:
### Solution

```javascript
function paginate(items, page, pageSize) {
  const totalItems = items.length;
  const totalPages = Math.max(1, Math.ceil(totalItems / pageSize));
  const safePage = Math.min(Math.max(1, page), totalPages);
  const start = (safePage - 1) * pageSize;

  return {
    items: items.slice(start, start + pageSize),
    page: safePage,
    totalPages,
    totalItems,
    hasPrev: safePage > 1,
    hasNext: safePage < totalPages,
  };
}
```

### How it works

`Math.ceil(total / pageSize)` gives the page count; `Math.max(1, ...)` keeps an empty list at one page rather than zero, so the UI can render "Page 1 of 1" instead of "Page 1 of 0".

Clamping with `Math.min(Math.max(1, page), totalPages)` handles both out-of-range directions, which is exactly what happens when a filter shrinks the result set while the user sits on page 5.

### Key points

- **The off-by-one:** `(page - 1) * pageSize`, not `page * pageSize`. The latter silently skips the first page of data.
- `slice` is safe past the end of an array — no bounds check needed for the final partial page.
- Clamping beats returning empty: a user who lands on a stale page sees the last page of data, not a blank table.
- In React, clamp during render rather than correcting `page` in an effect — the effect version renders once with the broken value first.

questions.template:
function searchItems(items, query, fields) {
  // Write your code here
}

questions.explanation:
### Solution

```javascript
function searchItems(items, query, fields) {
  const q = query.trim().toLowerCase();
  if (!q) return items;

  return items.filter((item) =>
    fields.some((field) => {
      const value = item[field];
      if (value == null) return false;
      return String(value).toLowerCase().includes(q);
    })
  );
}
```

### How it works

`filter` keeps an item when `some` field matches — "any field" is exactly `Array.prototype.some`. Normalizing the query once outside the loop avoids re-lowercasing it per item.

The `value == null` check uses loose equality deliberately: it catches both `null` and `undefined` in one comparison, which is the one place `==` is idiomatic.

### Key points

- **Return all items on an empty query**, don't return `[]`. Clearing the search box should restore the full list.
- **Trim before the emptiness check**, so a query of spaces behaves like an empty one.
- `String(value)` lets numeric fields match, which users expect when typing an id or quantity.
- Lowercase both sides. Case-sensitive search reads as broken.
- In a component, feed this a **debounced** query — see [Debounce](/coding/13) — and wrap the call in `useMemo`.

questions.template:
function chunk(array, size) {
  // Write your code here
}

questions.explanation:
### Solution

```javascript
function chunk(array, size) {
  if (size <= 0) return [];

  const out = [];
  for (let i = 0; i < array.length; i += size) {
    out.push(array.slice(i, i + size));
  }
  return out;
}
```

### How it works

Step the index by `size` rather than by one, slicing a window at each stop. `slice` clamps to the array length, so the final short chunk needs no special handling.

A declarative alternative:

```javascript
const chunk = (arr, size) =>
  size <= 0
    ? []
    : Array.from({ length: Math.ceil(arr.length / size) }, (_, i) =>
        arr.slice(i * size, i * size + size)
      );
```

### Key points

- Guard `size <= 0` first — without it the loop never advances and hangs the tab. In a timed test that is a lost question.
- An empty input yields `[]`, not `[[]]`.
- Chunks are shallow slices: the inner objects are shared references, not copies.

questions.template:
function validate(values, rules) {
  // Write your code here
}

questions.explanation:
### Solution

```javascript
function validate(values, rules) {
  const errors = {};

  for (const [field, fieldRules] of Object.entries(rules)) {
    const value = values[field];

    for (const rule of fieldRules) {
      let ok = true;

      switch (rule.type) {
        case "required":
          ok = value != null && String(value).trim() !== "";
          break;
        case "minLength":
          ok = String(value ?? "").length >= rule.value;
          break;
        case "pattern":
          ok = rule.value.test(String(value ?? ""));
          break;
        case "min":
          ok = Number(value) >= rule.value;
          break;
        case "max":
          ok = Number(value) <= rule.value;
          break;
        case "custom":
          ok = rule.validate(value, values);
          break;
      }

      if (!ok) {
        errors[field] = rule.message;
        break; // first failure wins — stop checking this field
      }
    }
  }

  return errors;
}
```

### How it works

Two loops: fields on the outside, that field's rules on the inside. The inner `break` after recording an error is what gives "first failing rule wins" — order your rules from most fundamental (`required`) to most specific (`minLength`, `pattern`) so the message the user sees is the useful one.

Passing `values` as the second argument to `custom` is what makes cross-field rules — "confirm password must match password" — expressible.

### Key points

- **Compute, don't store.** In a component, call this on every render: `const errors = validate(values, rules)`. Errors held in state drift out of sync with the values.
- **Separate validity from visibility.** `errors` is always current; a separate `touched` map decides whether to *show* a message. Otherwise the form is red before the user types.
- `String(value).trim()` makes `"   "` fail `required`, which is almost always what you want.
- `value ?? ""` guards against `undefined` for a field the user has not touched.
- An empty `errors` object is the validity signal — no separate `isValid` flag to keep in sync.

questions.template:
function formatRelativeTime(timestamp, now) {
  // Write your code here
}

questions.explanation:
### Solution

```javascript
function formatRelativeTime(timestamp, now) {
  const seconds = Math.floor((now - timestamp) / 1000);

  if (seconds < 60) return "just now";

  const units = [
    { label: "minute", secs: 60 },
    { label: "hour", secs: 3600 },
    { label: "day", secs: 86400 },
    { label: "week", secs: 604800 },
  ];

  // Walk from largest to smallest and take the first that fits.
  for (let i = units.length - 1; i >= 0; i--) {
    const { label, secs } = units[i];
    if (seconds >= secs) {
      const value = Math.floor(seconds / secs);
      return `${value} ${label}${value === 1 ? "" : "s"} ago`;
    }
  }

  return "just now";
}
```

### How it works

Convert to seconds once, then find the largest unit that divides into it at least once. Walking the table from the largest unit downward means the first match is the right granularity — 90000 seconds hits `day` before it can hit `hour`.

Pluralization is a single ternary on the computed value, which is why the unit labels are stored singular.

### Key points

- **`Math.floor`, not `Math.round`.** Rounding would report 90 seconds as "2 minutes ago", which reads as wrong.
- **Take `now` as a parameter** rather than calling `Date.now()` inside. That is what makes the function pure and testable — a function that reads the clock internally cannot be asserted against.
- The built-in `Intl.RelativeTimeFormat` does the localized version:

```javascript
new Intl.RelativeTimeFormat("en", { numeric: "auto" }).format(-5, "minute");
// "5 minutes ago"
```

- Weeks are the usual stopping point; past a month, an absolute date is more useful than "9 weeks ago".

questions.template:
function buildQueryString(params) {
  // Write your code here
}

function parseQueryString(str) {
  // Write your code here
}

questions.explanation:
### Solution

```javascript
function buildQueryString(params) {
  const parts = [];

  for (const [key, value] of Object.entries(params)) {
    if (value === null || value === undefined || value === "") continue;

    const values = Array.isArray(value) ? value : [value];
    for (const v of values) {
      parts.push(`${encodeURIComponent(key)}=${encodeURIComponent(v)}`);
    }
  }

  return parts.join("&");
}

function parseQueryString(str) {
  const clean = str.replace(/^\?/, "");
  if (!clean) return {};

  const out = {};

  for (const pair of clean.split("&")) {
    if (!pair) continue;

    const eq = pair.indexOf("=");
    const rawKey = eq === -1 ? pair : pair.slice(0, eq);
    const rawValue = eq === -1 ? "" : pair.slice(eq + 1);

    const key = decodeURIComponent(rawKey);
    const value = decodeURIComponent(rawValue);

    if (key in out) {
      out[key] = Array.isArray(out[key]) ? [...out[key], value] : [out[key], value];
    } else {
      out[key] = value;
    }
  }

  return out;
}
```

### How it works

Building normalizes every value to an array so the repeat-key case and the single-value case share one code path.

Parsing splits on the **first** `=` using `indexOf` rather than `split("=")`, because a base64 or encoded value can legitimately contain `=` and `split` would truncate it. The duplicate-key branch promotes a scalar to an array on second sight and appends thereafter.

### Key points

- **Skip empty values when building.** `?q=&page=` is noise; a cleared filter should leave the URL.
- **Everything parses back as a string.** `parseQueryString("page=2").page` is `"2"`, not `2` — coerce with `Number()` where you need it. This bites people restoring pagination state.
- `encodeURIComponent` (not `encodeURI`) is the right tool for a single key or value — it escapes `&`, `=`, and `?`.
- In real code, `new URLSearchParams(obj).toString()` does the whole job. Interviewers ask for the manual version to see whether you know what it handles.

questions.template:
function curry(fn) {
  // Write your code here
}

questions.explanation:
### Solution

```javascript
function curry(fn) {
  return function curried(...args) {
    // Enough arguments? Run the real function.
    if (args.length >= fn.length) {
      return fn.apply(this, args);
    }

    // Not yet — return a collector that remembers what we have so far.
    return function (...rest) {
      return curried.apply(this, [...args, ...rest]);
    };
  };
}
```

### How it works

`curried` is a **named function expression**, so it can call itself recursively without depending on the outer variable name.

Each partial call closes over `args`. When more arguments arrive, they're concatenated onto the previous ones and `curried` runs again with the fuller list. The recursion bottoms out the moment `args.length >= fn.length`.

`fn.length` is the count of **declared parameters before any default or rest parameter**. That's the detail that trips people up:

```javascript
((a, b) => 0).length;        // 2
((a, b = 1) => 0).length;    // 1 — defaults are not counted
((...args) => 0).length;     // 0 — rest is not counted
```

So currying a variadic function doesn't work — it fires immediately. Worth saying out loud.

### The follow-ups

**"Support placeholders," e.g. `curried(_, 2)(1)`:**

```javascript
const _ = Symbol('placeholder');

function curry(fn) {
  return function curried(...args) {
    const filled = args.filter((a) => a !== _).length;

    if (filled >= fn.length && !args.includes(_)) {
      return fn.apply(this, args);
    }

    return function (...rest) {
      const merged = args.map((a) => (a === _ && rest.length ? rest.shift() : a));
      return curried.apply(this, [...merged, ...rest]);
    };
  };
}
```

**"Why `apply(this, …)` and not just `fn(...args)`?"** — So a curried method keeps its receiver. Without it, `curry(obj.method)(x)` loses `this`.

**"What's the point?"** — Specialization: `const double = curry(multiply)(2)`. It shows up constantly in functional pipelines and in creating pre-configured handlers.

### Key points

- Closures are what hold the accumulated arguments between calls
- `fn.length` defines "enough" — and ignores defaults and rest params
- A named function expression lets the inner function recurse safely
- Currying (one argument at a time) is not the same as partial application (some arguments now, the rest later)

questions.template:
function twoSum(nums, target) {
  // Write your code here
}

questions.explanation:
### Solution

```javascript
function twoSum(nums, target) {
  const seen = new Map();   // value → index

  for (let i = 0; i < nums.length; i++) {
    const need = target - nums[i];

    // Check BEFORE inserting, so an element can't pair with itself
    if (seen.has(need)) return [seen.get(need), i];

    seen.set(nums[i], i);
  }

  return [];
}
```

### How it works

The brute-force version asks, for each element, *"is my complement somewhere in the rest of this array?"* — and answers it by searching, which is O(n) per element and O(n²) overall.

A hash map makes that search free. Walk once, and at each element ask the map whether the complement has **already been seen**. Because you only ever look backwards, you find each pair exactly once, at its second element.

**O(n) time, O(n) space.**

### Why check before inserting

If you inserted first, then `twoSum([3, 3], 6)` on the first `3` would find itself and return `[0, 0]`. Checking first means the map only ever contains *earlier* elements.

### The follow-ups

**"What if the array is sorted?"** — Then two pointers gives O(n) time and **O(1) space**, which is strictly better. See [/algorithm/3](/algorithm/3).

**"What if there are multiple answers?"** — Collect instead of returning early, and be careful about counting the same pair twice.

**"Three numbers instead of two?"** — Sort, then fix one element and run two pointers on the rest: O(n²). Skipping equal neighbours is what avoids duplicate triples.

### Key points

- Any "have I seen this before?" inside a loop is a hash-map signal
- Store the **index** as the value, since indices are what's being returned
- O(n) time costs O(n) space — name that trade-off out loud

questions.template:
function lengthOfLongestSubstring(s) {
  // Write your code here
}

questions.explanation:
### Solution

```javascript
function lengthOfLongestSubstring(s) {
  const seen = new Set();
  let left = 0;
  let best = 0;

  for (let right = 0; right < s.length; right++) {
    // Shrink from the left until the incoming char is no longer a duplicate
    while (seen.has(s[right])) {
      seen.delete(s[left]);
      left++;
    }

    seen.add(s[right]);
    best = Math.max(best, right - left + 1);
  }

  return best;
}
```

### How it works

The window `[left, right]` always holds a run of **distinct** characters. Extend it by moving `right`. If the new character is already inside the window, shrink from the left — deleting as you go — until it isn't. Then record the size.

`right - left + 1` is the window length. The `+ 1` is inclusive-bounds arithmetic and it is the most common bug in this pattern.

### Why this is O(n), not O(n²)

This is the follow-up they will ask, and it's worth being able to answer crisply:

> `left` never decreases. Across the whole run it advances at most `n` times in total, regardless of how those advances are distributed among iterations. So the nested `while` contributes O(n) overall, not O(n) per iteration. Total O(2n) = **O(n)**.

**Space is O(min(n, k))** where k is the alphabet size — the set can never exceed the number of distinct characters.

### The `"dvdf"` trap

A common wrong version jumps `left` straight to `lastIndex + 1` from a map. On `"dvdf"`, that can move `left` *backwards* if the stale index is behind the current window, corrupting the count. Either guard with `left = Math.max(left, lastIndex + 1)`, or use the `Set`-and-shrink version above, which cannot go wrong.

### The follow-ups

**"Return the substring, not the length."** — Track `bestLeft` when you update `best`, then `s.slice(bestLeft, bestLeft + best)`.

**"At most K distinct characters instead of zero repeats."** — Swap the `Set` for a `Map` of counts and shrink while `map.size > k`. Same skeleton — which is the point of learning the skeleton.

### Key points

- "Contiguous" plus "longest such that" means sliding window
- Choosing the right window state (`Set` here, count `Map` elsewhere) is the real work
- Be ready to explain the amortized O(n) argument

questions.template:
function isValid(s) {
  // Write your code here
}

questions.explanation:
### Solution

```javascript
function isValid(s) {
  const pairs = { ')': '(', ']': '[', '}': '{' };
  const stack = [];

  for (const char of s) {
    if (char === '(' || char === '[' || char === '{') {
      stack.push(char);
    } else {
      // Wrong opener on top, or nothing open at all (pop() gives undefined)
      if (stack.pop() !== pairs[char]) return false;
    }
  }

  return stack.length === 0;   // anything still open is invalid
}
```

### How it works

At every closing bracket, the only thing that matters is **the most recent unclosed opener**. That is exactly what a stack gives you in O(1), which is why this problem is a stack problem rather than a counting problem.

Push openers. On a closer, pop and verify the pair. Two failure modes fall out for free:

- **Popping an empty stack** returns `undefined`, which never equals a valid opener — so a stray `")"` fails immediately.
- **A non-empty stack at the end** means something was opened and never closed — hence the final length check.

That last check is the one candidates forget. Without it, `"((("` returns `true`.

**O(n) time, O(n) space.**

### The follow-ups

**"What if other characters can appear, like `"a(b)c"`?"** — Ignore anything that isn't a bracket. Change the `else` to only run for known closers.

**"Return the index of the first invalid bracket."** — Switch to an indexed `for` loop and push `{ char, index }` objects instead of bare characters.

**"What's the minimum number of insertions to make it valid?"** — Same traversal; count unmatched closers as you go and add the leftover stack size at the end.

### Key points

- "Matching", "nesting", "balanced" → stack
- In JavaScript a stack is **just an array** — `push`/`pop`, both O(1)
- Map closers to openers, not the other way round: you look up on the closer
- Never forget the final `stack.length === 0`

questions.template:
function binarySearch(arr, target) {
  // Write your code here
}

questions.explanation:
### Solution

```javascript
function binarySearch(arr, target) {
  let lo = 0;
  let hi = arr.length - 1;      // inclusive upper bound

  while (lo <= hi) {            // <= because lo === hi is still a valid window
    const mid = Math.floor((lo + hi) / 2);

    if (arr[mid] === target) return mid;

    if (arr[mid] < target) lo = mid + 1;   // mid is ruled out
    else hi = mid - 1;                     // mid is ruled out
  }

  return -1;
}
```

### How it works

Each comparison eliminates half of what remains, so the search finishes in about `log₂(n)` steps — roughly 20 for a million elements, 30 for a billion. **O(log n) time, O(1) space.**

### The three details that cause every bug

1. **`hi = arr.length - 1` pairs with `while (lo <= hi)`.** The other valid convention is `hi = arr.length` with `while (lo < hi)`. Both are correct; mixing them is not. Pick one and keep it.
2. **`mid + 1` and `mid - 1`, never bare `mid`.** You already tested `mid`, so leaving it inside the range means the window stops shrinking and you loop forever.
3. **The empty array works for free** — `hi` starts at `-1`, the loop never runs, and you return `-1`.

In JavaScript `Math.floor((lo + hi) / 2)` is safe. In a fixed-width-integer language you'd write `lo + Math.floor((hi - lo) / 2)` to avoid overflow — mentioning that you know *why* the idiom exists is a nice touch.

### The follow-ups

**"Return the first index `>= target` instead"** (lower bound):

```javascript
function lowerBound(arr, target) {
  let lo = 0;
  let hi = arr.length;        // exclusive — returning length is meaningful

  while (lo < hi) {
    const mid = Math.floor((lo + hi) / 2);
    if (arr[mid] < target) lo = mid + 1;
    else hi = mid;            // mid might BE the answer — don't discard it
  }

  return lo;
}
```

The entire difference is `hi = mid` rather than `hi = mid - 1`.

**"First and last occurrence of a duplicated value?"** — Two searches, one biased left and one biased right.

**"Rotated sorted array?"** — One half is always properly sorted. Determine which, check whether the target falls inside it, and recurse into the correct half.

### Key points

- "Sorted" or an O(log n) constraint essentially always means binary search
- Commit one boundary convention to muscle memory
- The generalization — searching an *answer space* rather than an array — is what unlocks the harder variants ([/algorithm/7](/algorithm/7))

