questions.description:
Given the root of a binary tree, return its values **grouped by level**, top to bottom, left to right.

A node is a plain object: `{ val, left, right }`.

### Example

```javascript
//     3
//    / \
//   9  20
//      / \
//     15  7

levelOrder(root);  // [[3], [9, 20], [15, 7]]
```

The tests build trees with a `node(val, left, right)` helper. Return `[]` for an empty tree.

Complete the implementation in the editor.

questions.template:
function levelOrder(root) {
  // Write your code here
}

questions.explanation:
### Solution

```javascript
function levelOrder(root) {
  if (!root) return [];

  const result = [];
  const queue = [root];

  while (queue.length) {
    const levelSize = queue.length;   // snapshot BEFORE we add any children
    const level = [];

    for (let i = 0; i < levelSize; i++) {
      const node = queue.shift();
      level.push(node.val);

      if (node.left) queue.push(node.left);
      if (node.right) queue.push(node.right);
    }

    result.push(level);
  }

  return result;
}
```

### How it works

Breadth-first search with a queue visits nodes in exactly the order you want: everything one step from the root, then everything two steps away, and so on.

The one trick is **capturing `levelSize` before the inner loop.** At the top of each outer iteration the queue contains precisely one level. Snapshot that count, drain exactly that many nodes, and every child you push lands *after* the snapshot — so it belongs to the next level. Without the snapshot the queue grows while you drain it and the levels smear into each other.

**O(n) time.** Space is **O(w)** where `w` is the widest level — for a balanced tree that's about n/2, so O(n).

### The `shift()` caveat

`Array.prototype.shift()` re-indexes the whole array: **O(n)**. Inside this loop that technically makes the traversal O(n²).

For interview-sized inputs it does not matter, and over-engineering it is the wrong instinct. But *say that you know*, then offer the fix — a read pointer instead of a mutation:

```javascript
const queue = [root];
let head = 0;

while (head < queue.length) {
  const levelSize = queue.length - head;
  for (let i = 0; i < levelSize; i++) {
    const node = queue[head++];
    // …
  }
}
```

That awareness is worth more than the optimization itself.

### The follow-ups

**"Bottom-up order?"** — Same traversal, then `result.reverse()`. Or `unshift` each level, though that's O(n) per insert.

**"Zigzag order?"** — Reverse every other level before pushing it. Keep a boolean and flip it each iteration.

**"Right-side view?"** — Push only the last value of each level.

**"Could you do this with DFS?"** — Yes: recurse with a `depth` argument and append into `result[depth]`. It produces the same output but uses O(h) stack space instead of O(w) queue space. On a wide shallow tree DFS wins on memory; on a deep narrow one BFS does.

### Key points

- "Level by level" or "shortest path" → BFS with a queue
- Snapshot the level size before pushing children — that's the whole pattern
- Guard the empty tree first
- Know the `shift()` cost even when you choose to live with it

questions.template:
function groupAnagrams(words) {
  // Write your code here
}

questions.explanation:
### Solution

```javascript
function groupAnagrams(words) {
  const groups = new Map();

  for (const word of words) {
    // Anagrams share the same sorted letters — a perfect group key
    const key = word.split('').sort().join('');

    if (!groups.has(key)) groups.set(key, []);
    groups.get(key).push(word);
  }

  return [...groups.values()];
}
```

### How it works

The entire problem is **choosing a key that is identical for anagrams and different for everything else.**

Sorted letters do exactly that: `"eat"`, `"tea"`, and `"ate"` all sort to `"aet"`. Once you see that, this becomes an ordinary bucketing problem.

A `Map` is the right container rather than a plain object for two reasons: it preserves **insertion order** for any key type (so groups come out in first-appearance order), and it avoids the prototype-pollution edge cases you get with `{}` — a word like `"constructor"` would collide with an inherited property.

**O(n · k log k)** where n is the word count and k the average word length — the `sort` inside the loop dominates. Space is O(n · k).

### The faster key

If they push on complexity, replace sorting with a character-count signature:

```javascript
function keyFor(word) {
  const counts = new Array(26).fill(0);
  for (const ch of word) counts[ch.charCodeAt(0) - 97]++;
  return counts.join('#');   // '#' separator so 1,11 ≠ 11,1
}
```

That's **O(n · k)** — you've traded the `log k` for a fixed 26-slot scan. The `'#'` separator matters: without it, counts of `[1, 11]` and `[11, 1]` both stringify to `"111"`.

Only reach for this if asked. Sorting is clearer, and clarity is worth more until complexity is explicitly challenged.

### The follow-ups

**"What about Unicode or uppercase?"** — The 26-slot version assumes lowercase ASCII. Normalize first, or fall back to sorting. Worth flagging as an assumption *before* they raise it.

**"Just check whether two words are anagrams."** — Compare frequency maps in O(n), with an early exit on differing lengths.

### Key points

- "Group by" → hash map; the skill is picking the key
- Sorted letters is the clear key; a count signature is the fast one
- `Map` over `{}` for insertion order and prototype safety
- State the assumption about the character set out loud

questions.template:
function merge(intervals) {
  // Write your code here
}

questions.explanation:
### Solution

```javascript
function merge(intervals) {
  if (intervals.length === 0) return [];

  // Sort by start — copy first so we don't mutate the caller's array
  const sorted = [...intervals].sort((a, b) => a[0] - b[0]);
  const result = [sorted[0]];

  for (let i = 1; i < sorted.length; i++) {
    const [start, end] = sorted[i];
    const last = result[result.length - 1];

    if (start <= last[1]) {
      // Overlaps (or touches) — extend the last interval
      last[1] = Math.max(last[1], end);
    } else {
      result.push([start, end]);
    }
  }

  return result;
}
```

### How it works

Unsorted, any interval could overlap any other — that's O(n²) comparisons. **Sorting by start** collapses the problem: once sorted, an interval can only ever overlap the one immediately before it in the output. So a single sweep suffices.

At each step there are exactly two cases:

- **`start <= last.end`** — they overlap or touch, so absorb it by pushing `last.end` outward.
- **Otherwise** — there is a genuine gap, so start a new interval.

`Math.max` on the merge is essential and easy to miss: `[[1, 10], [2, 3]]` must stay `[1, 10]`. Without the max you'd shrink it to `[1, 3]`.

**O(n log n)** — dominated by the sort. The sweep itself is O(n).

### Two details worth saying out loud

1. **`sort((a, b) => a[0] - b[0])`, never bare `sort()`.** The default comparator stringifies, so `[10, …]` sorts before `[9, …]`. This is the single most common JavaScript sorting bug.
2. **`[...intervals]` before sorting.** `sort` mutates in place; copying keeps the function pure. Interviewers notice this.

### The follow-ups

**"Insert a new interval into an already-sorted list."** — No sort needed: skip everything ending before the new start, merge everything overlapping, append the rest. O(n).

**"Find the minimum number of meeting rooms."** — Same family. Separate the starts and ends, sort both, and sweep with a counter — or use a min-heap of end times. The answer is the peak concurrent count.

**"How would you detect if any two intervals overlap at all?"** — Sort, then a single pass checking `start < previousEnd`. O(n log n), no merging required.

### Key points

- Intervals plus "overlap" almost always means **sort by start, then sweep**
- Always pass a numeric comparator to `sort`
- Copy before sorting to avoid mutating the input
- `Math.max` when extending — the contained-interval case depends on it

questions.template:
function moveZeroes(arr) {
  // Write your code here
}

questions.explanation:
### Solution

```javascript
function moveZeroes(arr) {
  let write = 0;   // next slot that should receive a non-zero value

  for (let read = 0; read < arr.length; read++) {
    if (arr[read] !== 0) {
      [arr[write], arr[read]] = [arr[read], arr[write]];
      write++;
    }
  }

  return arr;
}
```

### How it works

Two pointers moving in the **same direction**, at different speeds.

`read` visits every element. `write` only advances when a non-zero is placed. That gives you an invariant worth stating out loud:

> Everything before `write` is finished — non-zero and in original order. Everything between `write` and `read` is zeros.

When `read` finds a non-zero, swapping it into `arr[write]` sends whatever was there — necessarily a zero, or the element itself if the pointers coincide — out to `read`'s position. The zeros accumulate at the back automatically. No second pass needed.

**O(n) time, O(1) extra space.** The last test asserts the array was mutated in place rather than replaced.

### Why swap instead of overwrite-then-fill

The alternative is: copy non-zeros forward, then fill the tail with zeros.

```javascript
let write = 0;
for (const value of arr) if (value !== 0) arr[write++] = value;
while (write < arr.length) arr[write++] = 0;
```

Also correct and also O(n). The swap version does one pass instead of two, and generalizes to problems where the displaced value matters — like partitioning around a pivot. Either answer is fine; being able to compare them is the point.

### The follow-ups

**"Minimize the number of writes."** — The swap version writes on every non-zero even when nothing needs to move. Guard with `if (read !== write)` to skip the no-op swaps.

**"Move zeroes to the front instead."** — Same pattern walking from the right, with `write` starting at `arr.length - 1`.

**"Remove all instances of a given value, returning the new length."** — Identical write-pointer pattern; return `write` instead of the array.

### Key points

- "In place", "without extra space", "preserve order" → same-direction two pointers
- `write` marks the boundary between finished and unprocessed
- State the invariant out loud — it is what proves the code correct
- The whole family (dedupe sorted array, remove element, partition) is this one skeleton
